    <h2>Key Findings and Policy Recommendations</h2>
  </div>

  <h3>Summary of Key Findings</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Uniformly adequate performance, not excellence:</strong> All students score above 3.0, but nearly half (49.4%) are in the &ldquo;Average&rdquo; band (3.0&ndash;3.99). Only 26.8% achieve Excellent status.</li>
    <li><strong>Flat GPA progression:</strong> The near-zero delta (+0.018) between Previous GPA and HSC means the system is maintaining but not improving student outcomes over time.</li>
    <li><strong>Socio-economic factors show minimal impact:</strong> Income, parent education, internet access, and private tuition each show less than 0.1 GPA point difference across groups. All correlations are near zero.</li>
    <li><strong>Study hours and attendance don't predict outcomes:</strong> Correlations of -0.022 and -0.014 respectively mean these behavioral measures are not reliable levers.</li>
    <li><strong>Geographic disparities are the strongest signal:</strong> The 0.204 GPA gap between Barisal and Rangpur, and the 16.5pp excellence rate gap, represent the most actionable disparity in the data.</li>
    <li><strong>Public-private dynamics are district-specific:</strong> Dhaka's public schools significantly outperform private (+0.234), while Rajshahi shows the reverse (-0.173).</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>
  <div class="rec-grid">
    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>1. Prioritize Rangpur and Khulna for Intervention</h4>
      <p>These two divisions consistently rank lowest on HSC averages and excellent performance rates. Targeted resource allocation — teacher training, infrastructure upgrades, and supplementary academic programs — should focus here first. The gap with top-performing Barisal provides a measurable target.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>2. Investigate Unmeasured Quality Factors</h4>
      <p>Since standard socio-economic and behavioral variables explain virtually none of the outcome variance, the key drivers likely lie in classroom quality, teacher effectiveness, curriculum implementation, and exam preparation strategies. Commission school-level studies capturing these variables.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>3. Address the "Average Ceiling"</h4>
      <p>With 49.4% of students in the Average band and a flat GPA delta, the system is not lifting students toward excellence. Introduce differentiated instruction or enrichment programs to move the ~250 students near the Good/Excellent boundary (GPA 4.0&ndash;4.49) upward.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>4. Study District-Specific Best Practices</h4>
      <p>Barisal achieves 36.8% excellent rate despite having no obvious demographic advantages. Understanding what drives Barisal's success — whether school culture, teacher quality, community engagement, or pedagogical approaches — could yield replicable models.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>5. Re-evaluate Private Tuition Investment</h4>
      <p>Given the near-zero performance difference between students with and without private tuition (0.012 GPA), families are spending on tutoring with no measurable return. Public communication about effective study strategies may be more impactful.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>6. District-Specific School System Strategies</h4>
      <p>Rather than blanket public-vs-private policies, adopt district-specific strategies. In Dhaka, strengthen public schools (already outperforming). In Rajshahi, examine why public schools lag private by 0.173 GPA and address those specific gaps.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout success">
    <strong>Positive signal:</strong> The absence of a strong socio-economic gradient means the education system is, at a baseline level, providing equitable access to academic outcomes regardless of family background. The challenge is shifting the overall distribution upward — particularly converting "Average" performers into "Good" or "Excellent" ones, and closing the geographic gaps between top- and bottom-performing districts.
